window.NOTES = {"height":1400,"heightCm":22.9,"note":"Written by website/_build/add_notes.sh from notes_pages.json; edit that file, not this one.","pages":[{"alt":"The notebook's cover: a kraft-brown Muji double ring steno book with its printed label, and “Nebius × Nvidia” written faintly in pencil near the top","kind":"cover","src":"assets/notes/note-01.webp","tearTop":0.0415},{"alt":"Notebook page 1 of 12, dated October 4, 2026: my hackathon goals, the project question (reusing a policy trained on one robot on another), and terms to look up","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-02.webp","tearTop":0.0414,"transcript":"October 4, 2026\n\nToday, I'm beginning my work on the Nebius × Nvidia\n      hackathon.\n\nMy goals are straightforward. I want to learn about the\nNebius platform Nvidia models, & robot learning.\n\nFor my project I will be investigating how a policy trained\n& effective with one robot, can be reusd onto another robot\nfor the same task.\n\n          What is:?\n    a policy\n    freezing a trained policy\n    behaviour transfer\n    Nebius\n    Nvidia models & their uses\n    ...\n\n    [signature]"},{"alt":"Notebook page 2 of 12: sizing a VM, training a neural network, security groups and SSH keys, nvidia-smi, terminal vs shell, the ssh command","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-03.webp","tearTop":0.0586,"transcript":"When creating a VM we must consider;\n          CPU & RAM\n            GPU : Run simulation & training\n        Storage : Software, trained policies, recordings\n Price & Region : where it runs & what it costs\n\nA neural network turns observations into actions.\nWe can train it by;\n      1. Demonstration\n      2. Feedback (reward)\n\nSecurity Group: May this connection reach my VM\n  SSH Key : May this person log in (parsword)\n\nIP can change\nSSH identifies the person\n\n  nvidia-smi - GPU info TUI (System_{server} Management Interface)\n\n  Terminal displays text\n   Shell reads commands & launches programs\n\n  ssh = secured shell\n  -i - identity\n   miodrag@VM_PUBLIC_IP\n     ↑ my linux username\n     -v : add connection diagnostics"},{"alt":"Notebook page 3 of 12: SSHD, host keys, TOFU, tenants and projects, VRAM, Ubuntu, the kernel, NAT, TCP, ports, security groups, CIDR","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-04.webp","tearTop":0.0361,"transcript":"SSHD = Secured Shell Daemon; The process listening for, accepting,\n      and managing incoming SSH connections.\nSSH proves both directions\n\nThe servers host key proves to my mac that it's connecting to the\n      expected server - not an imposter.\n\nSSH both devices establish a pk and public key.\nTOFU\n\nTenant: A broad organizational boundary for cloud resources and access.\nProject: A grouping of cloud resources within a tenant\nVRAM: GPU ram\nUBUNTU: The Linux distribution running in the cloud VM\nOS Kernel: The OS core manging HW&SW. Mine is Linux.\nNAT: Network Address Translation;\n          maps addresses between network contexts\n      All devices have private address, router has public IPv4\n\nTCP = Transmission Control Protocol provides system for an ordered_{reliable} bytestream\n\n\"Port\" = a numbered transport endpoint. Standard TCP uses SSH on Port 22.\n\nSecurity Group: Firewall for VMs\n      Set of rules, stateless or stateful, managing which network\n            connections are allowed to enter OR leave the VM.\n\nCIDR = Classless Inter-Domain Routing (Public or Private).\n      /22, /32, /24. How many addresses does a rule apply to."},{"alt":"Notebook page 4 of 12: Ed25519, known_hosts, SHA256 fingerprints, the SSH handshake, Isaac Lab environments, USD, occupancy and voxel grids, .pt files","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-05.webp","tearTop":0.0625,"transcript":"Ed25519 : a digital signature algorithm\n\nKnown host public IPs are usually saved in\n          /Users/miodrag/.ssh/known_hosts\n———\n  SSH, Ed25519, SHA256\n\n  SSH is the overall connection process\n  Ed25519 handles signatures\n  SHA256 calculates the public key fingerprints\n\n① You run SSH on the Mac\n\n② SSH handshake begins: Mac & VM exchange info\n———\nEnvironment for Isaac Lab Simulation;\n      1. Environment ID\n      2. Python Config. & task code\n      3. Robot/Scene assets and physics settings\nusd = \"Universal Scene Description\"\n      can be a \".usd\" file, describing it's structure & properties.\n[diagram: “Robot” is circled, with an arrow to the .usd note]\n———\nOccupancy Grid } = { 2D } representation of occupied/not occ\nVoxel Grid     }   { 3D }\n———\nPyTorch saves weights or checkpoints as .pt or .pth files"},{"alt":"Notebook page 5 of 12: Python classes and imports, then four kinds of policy: rules, a proportional controller, a lookup table, MPC","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-06.webp","tearTop":0.0568,"transcript":"FrankaReachEnvCfg(ReachEnvCfg) means () is the superclass that ...() inherits [from?]\n\nfrom blah.blahblah.ab.cyxz import Blahblah\n    from this module → cyxz [circled]\n    import this class → Blahblah\n\n        def __post_init__\n            ↑ a function inside a class = a method\n———\nA policy can be many different things (5 examples);\n ① Handwritten rules:\n          If hand_position < target_position:\n              move_right()\n          ...\n ② A mathematicat formula; (This example is called a \"Proportional Controller\")\n          error = target_pos - hand_pos\n          movement = error * 0.1\n          } As the hand gets closer, the commanded movement gets smaller.\n ③ Lookup table\n          Target too far right: move right quickly\n          Target slightly right: move right slowly\n            At target: stop\n              [\"target_left\": \"move_left\",  ← stored answers\n               \"___\": \"___\",\n               ...\n ④ Optimization Procedure (for example MPC (Model Predictive Control))\n      (i) Predict a few steps ahead, several versions\n      (ii) Take one step of the best prediction path\n      (iii) Step 1 again."},{"alt":"Notebook page 6 of 12: a neural network as a policy, corruption and noise, play mode, observation groups, the concatenated input vector","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-07.webp","tearTop":0.0546,"transcript":"⑤ Neural Network\n      Robot Observations → NN with^{(policy)} trained weights → Actions done\n            (or given to controller to execute)\n\nCorruption = noise applied to Observation Group's terms\n            the Observation terms\n              they have observation values\nPlay = not training, rather just using.\n\nNoise = something like censor slightly off by 0.006... or something slightly off\n      vs simulated environment, Friction, mass, ....\n\nObservation group = all the data to be bundled/concatenated that is\n            then fed into a NN.\n\nInfo | Example Values | What it tells the policy\nJoint Positions | [0.50, -0.20] | How our joints are positioned on body ([per joint?])\nJoint Velocities | [0.10, 0.00] | How fast they are moving\nTarget Position (absolute) | [0.40, 0.10, 0.30] | Where we want the tool tip to go (may also include orientation)\nPrevious Action | [0.02, -0.01] | What command the policy issued last\n        ↓ Concatenate\n[0.50, -0.20, 0.10, 0.00, 0.40, 0.10, 0.30, 0.02, -0.01]\n        ↑ order matters, as NN is trained to expect such.\n        ↓\n      N.N (the [policy?] is the model based on\n        ↓\n      Next action\n[diagram: an arrow loops from “Next action” back to the start of the list]\n\nThe policy is a decision making model, that recieves the bundle"},{"alt":"Notebook page 7 of 12: observations vs the policy, training and inference, USD, robot joints (stiffness, damping, limits), torque, RSL-RL, PPO","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-08.webp","tearTop":0.0504,"transcript":"observations.policy... is different from the policy\n    ↑ the observations group\n                                    ↑ makes decision with N.N. brain\n\nThe training algorithm specifies how experience should be used to learn\n\nProducing an output is called Inference\n\nEnvironment\n\nUSD = universal scene description\n\n                Robot Joints\n\n  Stiffness: How hard we try to rotate\n  Damping: The 'softening' of in or out (can be electric reverse pushed to counter velocity)\n  Velocity Limit: ____\n  Effort Limit: Physical limit of our hardware we allow based on; stiffness & Δdistance from target angle\n\n  An requested angle is a goal, not an instant change in angle.\n  May have to; ① Overcome gravity,\n               ② Inertia\n               ③ Load\n               ④ Collision\n\nTorque = how hard you're trying to turn something\nRSL = Robotic Systems Lab, RL; Reinforcement Learning\nPPO = Proximal Policy Optimization : The training algorithm"},{"alt":"Notebook page 8 of 12: RSL-RL, the actor and critic, PPO updates, how rewards are scored, a reward term in reach_env_cfg.py","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-09.webp","tearTop":0.0479,"transcript":"RSL-RL = ~~Software~~/Library\n      PPO is an algotithm utilized in it.\n\nObs → Actor^{(NN/Policy)} → Actions & Reward from environment\n      Critic ← how well I thought we'd do - adjusted by\n               PPO per xSteps (like 24/[?] during\n\n  Run X Steps (like 24 or 10) → Train\n              ↑ default & my example\n\n      Actor does the steps\n      Critic predicts TOTAL_{over 24 or 10} Steps,\n\nRewards can be;\n      How far from target?: -1: 1cm, -2: 2cm, -3: 3cm...\n      How long can we \"hold\"?: +2: 2seconds, +3: 3seconds..\n\nreach_env_cfg.py :\n\nRewTerm(). = construct a reward term configuration\n\nend_effector_position_tracking = RewTerm(  ← our name for specific scoring term\n      func=mdp.position_command_error,  ← reference to function that calculates distance. called later\n      weight=-0.2,  ← multiply () result by -0.2\n      params={\"asset_cfg\": ___,\n        \"command_name\": \"ee_pose\",  ← use target supplied by 'ee_pose' command\n      })"},{"alt":"Notebook page 9 of 12: a worked position-error example, the action-rate penalty, how the environment's score rewards and penalizes","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-10.webp","tearTop":0.0429,"transcript":"example position error;\n\n      Target: (0.50, 0.00, 0.30) metres\n  Actual Hand: (0.40, 0.00, 0.30) metres\n\n      Distance = 0.10 metres\n  Weighted contribution = -0.2 × 0.10 = -0.02\n— — — — —\naction_rate = RewTerm(\n      func=mdp.action_rate_l2,\n      weight=0.0001,\n)                         ↑ will essentially\n                            give this current action change\n                            minus previous, ^2.\n\n      like current requested action = [0.20, 0.10, ...]\n                         previous = [0.50, 0.10, ...]\n                         ↑ larger    ↑ target positions, not \"move this much\"\n[diagram: the two lists are circled together]\n      So previous was more\n      significant. lets end up with positive (^2)\n\nThe score given by the environment, to help the robot based\n      on good things it's doing (such as being close to the target) &\n      bad things like changing [comments?] to drastically (\"penalties\" is the term)\nThis encourages smoother [comments?]."},{"alt":"Notebook page 10 of 12: checkpoints and iterations, Isaac-Reach-Franka-v0, steps vs iterations vs updates, std, .pt files, installing on the VM","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-11.webp","tearTop":0.0136,"transcript":"checkpoint = saved snapshot of training (saved state of policy weights)\niter = rounds of collect experience → run PPO updates (NOT one step. Per TRAIN.)\nMine is called Isaac-Reach-Franka-v0\n               ↑ labs/ software eco-system prefix\n                     ↑ just reach task, with Robot model Franka Panda\n\nIntended task: Franka Panda reaching\n\nTraining Framework: RSL-RL, using PPO\n\n  Simulation Steps: How many times a robot observes, acts, & advances\nTraining iterations: How many collect-and-update rounds\n  Optimizer updates: Individual weight adjustments within each round/iter\n\n  std = Action-distributed spread. Controls the spread of sampled actions\n  ↑ \"standard deviation\"\n\n  .pt = pytorch (or PyTorch)\n———\nto install onto the VM, we need 3 things/parts;\n      1. Prepare python environment (Place for the projects Py pkgs,\n      2. Install dependancies required for IsaacLab (5.1 sim & PyTorch\n                                                  ↑ run & train N.N.s\n      3. Install Isaac lab (Robotics Framework)\n            with RSL-RL      ↑ containing our reaching environment(s)\n            ↑ lab installs for us,   and training scripts\n              uses PyTorch"},{"alt":"Notebook page 11 of 12: a map of the Nebius VM, from Isaac Sim, Isaac Lab and RSL-RL to train.py, play.py and the project's own files","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-12.webp","tearTop":0.0436,"transcript":"[diagram: a tree drawn with brackets and arrows, laid out here as an indented list]\nNebius VM\n├ Our Python Environment\n  ├ Python 3.11\n  ├ Isaac Sim ← Simulate the robots, table, gravity, collisions, joint movement; render images when needed\n  ├ Isaac Lab ← configure setup of our reaching task into sim; which robot, targets, observations, actions, rewards, and resets, Connects the task to learning software.\n      Sim ⟷_{lab} RSL-RL\n  └ PyTorch + RSL-RL ← actor, critic NNs, PPO\n  environment. → Isaac Lab\n├ Isaac Lab source folder\n  ├ environment configurations\n    ├ Shared reaching configuration - (table, target sampling, observations group, rewards, episode duration)\n    ├ Franka-specific reaching config - (robot, robot-specific joint commands, tool-body names)\n    └ UR10-specific reaching config - unique assets; unique geometry & physical proper.\n  └ Training and playback scripts\n    ├ train.py ┐ both   initialize (set up ^{actor} critic, colled exp, run PPO, save checkpoints)\n    └ play.py  ┘        environment (run policy w/o updating weights, we will watch/record what happens (video, data ← ^{for} report))\n      the network sizes & learning settings come from config files such as rsl_rl_ppo_cfg.py\n└ Our Project files\n  ├ checkpoint.pt ← saved numerical state; {actors & critics learned weights, optimizer (PPO) state (+ metadata such as training iter.)}\n  ├ Adapter cote\n  └ experiment results ← folder of things like ppt, videos, excel... whatever. graphs... run settings... logs...\n      measured target errors, success rates [under?] our defined criteria..."},{"alt":"Notebook page 12 of 12, the last page and nearly blank: the ssh command for the VM, and python3 --version","kind":"page","src":"assets/notes/note-13.webp","tearTop":0.0593,"transcript":"ssh -i ~/.ssh/nebius_robot miodrag@ VMs ^{[infer(nal)?]} public IPv4 (no CIDR)\n\npython3 --version : check python"}],"width":929,"widthCm":15.2};
